// A borrowed reference to a generic instantiation. Marked xfail: '&Box[i64]'
// does not parse as a type.
//
// This used to be filed as one defect with '*Box[i64]' and '[2; Box[i64]]' --
// "an instantiation is not a type inside a composite type" -- and it is not.
// Those two were a name-resolution question, and generic-success now runs them
// alongside '?Box[i64]'. This one never reaches that question, because the
// parse has already gone elsewhere.
//
// A borrow parses its operand without suffixes and then re-applies them to the
// borrow, so that '&v[i]' is the '`&[]`' operator on v rather than a borrow of
// an element, and '&Point[1, 2]' allocates a literal. '&Box[i64]' takes that
// same path: it parses as an index applied to '&Box', a reference to the
// generic itself. Name resolution finds a well-formed reference type there and
// leaves it alone, so the enclosing '[i64]' is read as a type literal of
// '&Box' whose one field value is the type i64 -- hence ErrorNotTyped
// "Expected a typed expression" at the instantiation, and then "Expected a
// type" at the parameter, since what is left is an expression.
//
// So the fix is a precedence rule, not a predicate: '&' followed by a name that
// resolves to a generic has only one possible reading, and the borrow chain
// would have to yield to it. That is the same parseAmper suffix re-application
// that ref-field-borrow pins, and it is a language decision about how far a
// borrow reaches.
//
// The consequence still reaches past this group: it is why 'union' matches
// Option and Result by value rather than by reference, and it blocks any
// generic collection whose methods take '&self'.
//
// When this scenario starts passing, drop the xfail and fold it into
// generic-success as a printed fact.

struct Box[T] {
  v T
}

fn peek(b &Box[i64]) i64 {
  b.v
}
